# hyprpad — make the REAL Steam Controller invisible to Steam, on either
# transport: the puck/dongle (USB 28de:1304) and the controller's own Bluetooth
# radio (BLE 28de:1303).
#
# WHY
# ---
# hyprpad presents Steam a *virtual* Valve controller on /dev/uhid and streams
# the real puck into it (docs/design/uhid-relay.md). For that to be worth
# anything Steam must not also see the real puck: otherwise every press arrives
# twice — once from the fake, once from the real device — and Steam lists two
# controllers (risk R10).
#
# Steam and hyprpad run as the SAME user, so no group or ACL can separate them.
# The split is made in two halves:
#
#   1. THIS RULE takes the puck's hidraw nodes away from every unprivileged
#      process, the session user included. Nothing running as you can open them.
#   2. A tiny root broker (hyprpad-broker.socket / .service) opens them on
#      request and passes the open descriptors to the unprivileged hyprpad
#      daemon over a unix socket with SCM_RIGHTS.
#
# Valve's own /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-steam-input.rules hands `uaccess` to every
# 28de hidraw node — line 11 matches KERNELS=="000[356]:28DE:*", so it covers the
# Bluetooth controller (bus 0005) just as it covers the USB dongle. That is what
# makes the real controller reachable today on both transports — and, helpfully,
# it is also what makes hyprpad's *virtual* 28de:1302 / 28de:12f0 node reachable
# with no rule of our own. This file must therefore undo Valve's grant for the
# two REAL identities only, and leave the fake alone. It does: the matches below
# pin 1304 (USB) and 1303 (Bluetooth), and the virtual device is neither.
#
# BOTH TRANSPORTS, AND WHY EACH IS MATCHED DIFFERENTLY
# ----------------------------------------------------
# The dongle is a USB device, so ATTRS{idVendor}/ATTRS{idProduct} walk up to its
# USB parent and one rule covers all five of its hidraw nodes.
#
# The Bluetooth controller has no USB parent to walk to. A BlueZ-created HID
# device is parented to /devices/virtual/misc/uhid (drivers/hid/uhid.c), so there
# is no bluetooth device in the chain either and no ATTRS{address} anywhere.
# `ENV{HID_ID}` is not an option: a hidraw node carries NO HID_* properties of
# its own. Verified read-only on this machine —
#
#     $ udevadm info /dev/hidraw13
#     P: /devices/virtual/misc/uhid/0005:28DE:1303.000E/hidraw/hidraw13
#     E: SUBSYSTEM=hidraw
#     E: TAGS=:uaccess:seat:          <- Valve's line 11, the thing to undo
#     (no HID_ID, no HID_NAME, no ID_VENDOR_ID)
#
#     $ udevadm info -a -n /dev/hidraw13
#       looking at parent device '…/0005:28DE:1303.000E':
#         KERNELS=="0005:28DE:1303.000E"
#         SUBSYSTEMS=="hid"
#
# So the hook is the parent HID device's kernel name, which the kernel formats as
#   dev_set_name(&hdev->dev, "%04X:%04X:%04X.%04X", bus, vendor, product, id)
# (hid-core.c:3061) — bus 0005 = BUS_BLUETOOTH, 1303 =
# USB_DEVICE_ID_STEAM_CONTROLLER_IBEX_BLE (hid-ids.h:1390). The trailing .%04X is
# an instance counter that only ever increases, and BlueZ destroys and recreates
# the device on every disconnect (src/shared/uhid.c:546-563), so it MUST be
# globbed — the node comes back as .000F, .0010, … and the hidrawN number can
# change too.
#
# This is the same shape Valve's own line 11 uses, which is also the proof it
# reaches uhid-created nodes at all: hyprpad's fake (0003:28DE:1302.NNNN, no USB
# parent) picks up its ACL from that line and nothing else.
#
# WHY THE NUMBER IS 72  (this is the load-bearing detail)
# ------------------------------------------------------
# `TAG+="uaccess"` does not itself grant anything. It only sets a tag. The ACL is
# applied much later, by systemd's /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/73-seat-late.rules:
#
#     TAG=="uaccess|xaccess-*", ENV{MAJOR}!="", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"
#
# and `man udev` is explicit that RUN entries are "executed after processing of
# all the rules for the event", with the list only clearable wholesale by
# `RUN=""` / `RUN:=""`. So once 73-seat-late.rules has seen the tag and queued
# the builtin, removing the tag afterwards changes nothing — the ACL is applied
# regardless, and an ACL beats the mode bits, so even MODE:="0600" would leave
# `user:<you>:rw-` on the node.
#
# The window for `TAG-="uaccess"` is therefore:
#
#     after   60-steam-input.rules   (Valve adds the tag)
#     after   70-uaccess.rules       (systemd's own tag adders)
#     BEFORE  73-seat-late.rules     (the tag is consumed here)
#
# 72 sits in that window. 71 would work too (71-seat.rules does not touch
# uaccess) but 72 leaves room. **75, 95 or 99 would NOT work** — which is why the
# W12-era reference rule scripts/99-hyprpad-claim-puck.rules is superseded by
# this one. Verify the local ordering with:
#
#     grep -rn uaccess /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/7*.rules
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT MATCHED
# --------------------------------
# The controller's `input`/`event` nodes, on EITHER transport. hyprpad reads the
# controller over hidraw and only over hidraw; the evdev side is left exactly as
# the system configures it.
#
# Over the dongle there is normally nothing there to hide: the puck's
# vendor-collection descriptor produces no evdev node at all on this machine.
#
# Over Bluetooth there IS something there — the descriptor's two lizard
# collections (report 0x40 Mouse, 0x41 Keyboard) produce
# "Steam Ctrl (BT) … Mouse" and "… Keyboard" evdev nodes, and Valve's
# 60-steam-input.rules line 14 exposes them. They are still left alone, on
# purpose:
#
#   * the daemon DISABLES lizard mode (src/lizard.rs) within a second of
#     starting, after which those nodes exist but emit nothing — the firmware is
#     no longer generating the reports behind them. Hiding a node that produces
#     no events buys nothing;
#   * they are not what Steam binds a controller from. Steam reads the vendor
#     hidraw channel, which is what the clauses below take away;
#   * and a udev rule cannot be undone as cheaply as a feature report can. If
#     hyprpad is not running, those nodes are the ONLY thing making the
#     controller usable at all — leaving them alone is what keeps a machine with
#     a stopped daemon merely lizard-y rather than inert.
#
# If a future firmware grows an evdev node that matters, hiding it is a separate
# decision and not this file's business.
#
# `MODE`/`OWNER`/`GROUP` use the `:=` final-assignment operator so no later rule
# can widen them back.
#
# INSTALL
#     sudo install -Dm644 packaging/udev/72-hyprpad-puck.rules \
#          /etc/udev/rules.d/72-hyprpad-puck.rules
#     sudo udevadm control --reload
#     sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=hidraw
#
# VERIFY
#     # which nodes are the controller's, and what udev thinks their parents are
#     udevadm info -a -n /dev/hidraw7 | grep -E 'idVendor|idProduct|KERNELS'
#     # or, for every one of them at once, whichever transport they are on:
#     grep -l 'HID_ID=000[35]:000028DE:0000130[34]' /sys/class/hidraw/*/device/uevent
#     # the controller's nodes must be root-only, with NO '+' (no ACL)
#     ls -l /dev/hidraw*
#     getfacl /dev/hidraw7          # expect only user::rw- / group::--- / other::---
#     # and the fake must still carry an ACL for you
#     getfacl /dev/hidrawN          # the 28de:1302 or 28de:12f0 node
#     # hyprpad's own summary of all of the above, including the BT node:
#     hyprpad setup --check
#
#     # syntax, without installing anything:
#     udevadm verify packaging/udev/72-hyprpad-puck.rules
#
# REVERT
#     sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/72-hyprpad-puck.rules
#     sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=hidraw

ACTION=="remove", GOTO="hyprpad_puck_end"
SUBSYSTEM!="hidraw", GOTO="hyprpad_puck_end"

# 1. THE DONGLE — USB 28de:1304.
#
# Matched the way src/hidraw.rs matches it: the vendor and product of the USB
# device the hidraw node hangs off. The puck publishes one interface per pairing
# slot plus a dongle-control interface, and ATTRS{} walks up to the shared USB
# parent, so a single rule covers all five however they are numbered this boot.
ATTRS{idVendor}=="28de", ATTRS{idProduct}=="1304", \
    TAG-="uaccess", OWNER:="root", GROUP:="root", MODE:="0600"

# 2. BLUETOOTH — BLE 28de:1303, the 2026 controller's own radio.
#
# Matched on the parent HID device's kernel name, because there is no USB parent
# and no HID_ID property to match on — see "BOTH TRANSPORTS" above for the
# udevadm output this is read off. Exactly ONE hidraw node exists here, not five
# ("There is only one BLE HID interface", hid-steam.c:1618-1621).
#
# The id is exact rather than a glob: 0x1303 is pinned in mainline, so there is
# no reason to leave 13?? open. It deliberately does NOT match 1302 (the wired
# controller, which is also hyprpad's own fake identity) or 0005:28DE:11??
# (the 2015 controller). Only the instance suffix is globbed, and it has to be.
KERNELS=="0005:28DE:1303.*", \
    TAG-="uaccess", OWNER:="root", GROUP:="root", MODE:="0600"

LABEL="hyprpad_puck_end"
